---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ConverterPageFrame from '../components/ConverterPageFrame.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/FaqList.astro';
import { ARCHIVE_CONVERTER } from '../config/archive-converters';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';

const converter = ARCHIVE_CONVERTER;
const faq = [
  { question: 'Are my archive contents uploaded?', answer: 'No. 7Z, RAR, ZIP, TAR, TAR.GZ/TGZ and supported GZIP conversion runs in a browser worker on your device. The source archive is not sent to a conversion server.' },
  { question: 'Which archive formats are supported in this preview?', answer: '7Z and supported RAR4/RAR5 are input formats through a lazy-loaded WASM decoder. ZIP and TAR are native input/output families. TAR.GZ/TGZ and GZIP depend on browser gzip stream support. Public outputs remain ZIP, TAR and TAR.GZ.' },
  { question: 'Does converting an archive change the files inside it?', answer: 'The converter reads regular entry bytes in memory and writes them into a new container. File contents are not intentionally transformed, although archive-specific metadata that has no equivalent in the target format may not be preserved.' },
  { question: 'What safety limits are applied?', answer: 'The preview limits archive entry count, individual expanded entry size, total expanded data, source file size, and batch size. Unsafe paths, encrypted archives, links, special entries and corrupt container metadata are rejected.' },
  { question: 'Are encrypted ZIP, RAR or 7Z files supported?', answer: 'No. Password-protected archives are rejected instead of producing incomplete or misleading output.' },
];
const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: converter.h1,
  url: `${SITE.origin}/archive-converter/`,
  applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Any',
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
};
---
<SiteLayout title={converter.title} description={converter.description} schema={schema}>
  <ConverterPageFrame converter={converter} />
  <section class="content-wide-section white">
    <div class="site-container prose-content">
      <h2>Browser-first archive conversion</h2>
      <p>Open 7Z and RAR or convert between ZIP, TAR and TAR.GZ/TGZ without putting archive contents into a server-side upload queue. Single-file GZIP can also be moved into ZIP or TAR. Parsing, decompression, integrity checks and repacking happen in a dedicated browser worker so the main interface remains responsive.</p>
      <div class="callout"><strong>Preview safety policy</strong><p>Source files are signature-checked before admission. During parsing, the engine validates native container integrity where available, rejects unsafe paths and unsupported special entries, and caps expanded data at 2,000 entries, 100 MB per file, and 250 MB total.</p></div>
      <h2>What the upgraded preview supports</h2>
      <ul class="content-checklist">
        <li><strong>7Z → ZIP / TAR / TAR.GZ</strong> through a pinned libarchive WebAssembly decoder that loads only when a 7Z source is actually converted.</li>
        <li><strong>RAR4 / RAR5 → ZIP / TAR / TAR.GZ</strong> through the same lazy WASM path. Password-protected RAR is rejected.</li>
        <li><strong>ZIP → TAR / TAR.GZ</strong> with CRC verification and selectable Store, Balanced, or Maximum compression when ZIP is the destination.</li>
        <li><strong>TAR → ZIP / TAR.GZ</strong> with TAR checksum validation plus PAX extended paths and GNU long-name compatibility.</li>
        <li><strong>TAR.GZ / TGZ → ZIP / TAR</strong> when native gzip streams are available.</li>
        <li><strong>GZIP / GZ → ZIP / TAR</strong> for single-file gzip streams while recovering the original base filename from the source name.</li>
        <li>Batch queue, cancel, retry semantics, per-file downloads and ZIP packaging reuse the same shared converter platform as Image and Data.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Lazy WASM, not a server conversion</h2>
      <p>The 7Z/RAR compatibility layer is pinned to a reviewed libarchive-wasm build. During the site build, its JavaScript, WASM binary and MIT license are downloaded from a fixed upstream commit and verified by Git blob hash. The deployed files are then served as static ConvertAnyFile assets. Normal ZIP, TAR and GZIP users do not initialize this runtime.</p>
      <h2>Compatibility boundary</h2>
      <p>This preview deliberately does not promise exact preservation of symbolic links, hard links, ownership IDs, ACLs, extended attributes, sparse files or vendor-specific metadata. Unsupported links and special entries are rejected instead of silently disappearing from the converted archive. For deployment artifacts or backups that depend on exact metadata, keep the original archive and verify the converted output with the destination tool.</p>
      <p>7Z and RAR are currently <strong>input-only</strong>. ConvertAnyFile does not advertise 7Z or RAR output until a writer path has its own compatibility, fidelity and browser test matrix.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><FaqList items={faq} title="Archive converter FAQ" intro="How local 7Z, RAR, ZIP, TAR, TAR.GZ and GZIP conversion works in the upgraded preview." /></div></section>
</SiteLayout>
